  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "A rally in the sixth inning finally broke a tie that Atlanta had held since the second.",
    "what_this_means": "Molina gave the Giants five solid innings, holding Atlanta to three runs on six hits with six strikeouts. The bullpen did the rest. Foley, Sanmartin, and Smith combined for 3.1 scoreless innings with four strikeouts, nothing wasted. But for five innings, the lineup's 11 hits produced almost nothing. They left two runners on base in the critical second inning and went empty again in the fourth. Then Cox delivered the separation they needed. Two runs knocked in, the power that made the early chances matter retroactively. This is a team that wins through patience and one swing. It doesn't look dominant. It holds until someone converts, and last night Cox was that person. Jonah Cox made broad production count.",
    "what_to_watch": "Pittsburgh brings Paul Skenes (3.79 ERA) tonight at 3:40 PT. Webb goes for the Giants in what shapes as a matchup where early run support will matter. Skenes has allowed just 3.1 runs per nine innings, and the Pirates' offense ranks bottom-five in the division.",
